# Inslogic TPU 95A — FAST / high-flow experiment — Prusa MK3S+, 0.4 nozzle
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS AN EXPERIMENT, not a validated profile. It exists to answer one
# question before any filament is bought: on this printer, is the 1.2 mm3/s
# ceiling a property of the FILAMENT or of the PRINTER?
#
# Parent is NinjaTek Cheetah TPU, not Generic FLEX. That is the whole point:
#
#   Generic FLEX / Ultrafuse TPU-95A ... 1.2 mm3/s  -> 13 mm/s at 0.45 x 0.2
#   NinjaTek Cheetah TPU (95A) ......... 4.0 mm3/s  -> 44 mm/s
#   Overture High Speed TPU ............ 4.0 mm3/s  -> 44 mm/s
#   (reference) PLA on this printer .... 15  mm3/s  -> 167 mm/s
#
# 44 mm/s is essentially this machine's PLA PERIMETER speed (perimeter_speed = 45
# in the 0.2mm QUALITY @MK3 profile), so perimeter-speed parity with PLA is the
# realistic target. Infill parity is not - PLA infill runs 80 mm/s here.
#
# Cheetah also raises filament_retract_length to 1.5 mm where Generic FLEX uses 0.
# That is inherited deliberately and is half of why the fast grades run fast.
#
# WHAT TO EXPECT: Inslogic TPU 95A is a STANDARD 95A grade, not a high-flow one.
# Its TDS tops out at 230 C in the 80-120 mm/s band. It may well not sustain
# 4.0 mm3/s. That is the useful result either way:
#   - If it prints clean, the old 1.2 ceiling was the profile, not the material.
#   - If it under-extrudes or the Bondtech grinds, the material is the limit and a
#     genuine high-flow grade (Cheetah / Overture High Speed) is worth buying.
# Watch for: gaps in perimeters, a thinning wall, or filament dust in the extruder.
# Grinding is messy, not damaging - stop the print and clear the gears if it starts.
inherits = NinjaTek Cheetah TPU
filament_vendor = Inslogic
filament_type = FLEX
filament_diameter = 1.75
filament_density = 1.23
filament_spool_weight = 1000
temperature = 225
first_layer_temperature = 230
bed_temperature = 50
first_layer_bed_temperature = 50
cooling = 1
fan_always_on = 1
min_fan_speed = 100
max_fan_speed = 100
bridge_fan_speed = 100
disable_fan_first_layers = 3
filament_max_volumetric_speed = 4
filament_notes = "Inslogic TPU 95A, FAST/high-flow EXPERIMENT — TDS rev. 12.02.2024.\n\nPURPOSE: find out whether the 1.2 mm3/s flow ceiling on this printer's TPU\nprofiles comes from the FILAMENT or from the PRINTER, before buying a genuine\nhigh-flow grade. Not a validated profile.\n\nPARENT: NinjaTek Cheetah TPU (a real 95A high-speed grade, 4.0 mm3/s, and it\nraises retraction to 1.5 mm where Generic FLEX uses 0). NOT Generic FLEX.\n\nWHY 225 C (vs 210 C on the standard 'Inslogic TPU 95A'):\nFlow is going from 1.2 to 4.0 mm3/s - 3.3x the material through the same melt\nzone per second - which needs more heat to keep up. 225 C sits just under\nInslogic's stated 230 C maximum. First layer 230 C, at that maximum, for\nadhesion. Cheetah's own parent runs 240 C but that is a different polymer;\nstaying inside the Inslogic TDS matters more than matching the parent.\n\nFAN 100 % is from the TDS and is correct for TPU - it does not warp.\nBED 50 C is the TDS floor and matches Prusa's *FLEX* base.\nUse the TEXTURED PEI sheet, per the TDS. On smooth PEI, TPU can bond hard\nenough to damage the sheet.\n\nNOTE ON THE CEILING: 4.0 mm3/s is a ceiling, not a target, but the print profile\nwill actually reach it - infill at 80 mm/s demands 7.2 mm3/s at 0.45 x 0.2, so\nthe cap is what governs infill. This profile therefore genuinely exercises 4.0.\n\nFAILURE MODES TO WATCH: gaps or thinning in perimeters (under-extrusion), or\nfilament dust around the Bondtech gears (grinding). Both mean the material cannot\nsustain the flow - stop, clear the gears, and treat it as evidence that a real\nhigh-flow grade is the fix rather than a profile change.\n\nUNTESTED - built 30 Jul 2026."
